# Rust Style and Concurrency for COSMIC Applet

## Naming and Types
- Use descriptive names for messages and state (e.g., `TimeEntry`, `idle_started_at`).
- Prefer enums to represent UI/IO states over ad-hoc strings.
- Avoid `any`-like erasure; keep types explicit and clear.

## Control Flow & Errors
- Use early returns and guard clauses for clarity.
- Propagate errors meaningfully; log with context. Avoid swallowing errors silently.

## Threading & Wayland
- Wayland types (`Connection`, `EventQueue`, `QueueHandle`, protocol objects) are thread-affine. Create and use them on the same thread.
- Offload Wayland event dispatch to a dedicated thread using `IdleMonitor::spawn(...)`. Do not attempt to pump Wayland from the UI thread.
- Communicate back to the UI using `std::sync::mpsc` (or `crossbeam-channel`) with non-blocking `try_recv` in `Message::Tick`.

## Applet Instance Safety
- Keep per-instance state inside `Window` and avoid global mutable singletons.
- For IDs (`Id`, widget `Id`s), generate per instance and never reuse across processes.

## Performance
- Keep `subscription` tick handlers lightweight; drain channels and return.
- Avoid blocking `std::process::Command::output()` in the UI thread for long operations; consider async or background threads if it becomes slow.

## UI
- Size the panel button with `autosize` and avoid fixed pixel assumptions; use `suggested_size` and `suggested_padding` from `Core`.
- Close the popup on successful action to keep UX responsive.